1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,953
CloudCart has a new version, ready to ship.

2
00:00:03,503 --> 00:00:05,658
Would you send it straight to every customer?

3
00:00:06,208 --> 00:00:09,323
Of course not. It travels through safe stages first.

4
00:00:09,873 --> 00:00:14,523
Today you learn those stages, who approves each move, and how to undo a mistake.

5
00:00:15,073 --> 00:00:19,148
That is environments, approvals, release risk, rollback, and maintenance.

6
00:00:21,198 --> 00:00:23,011
First, what is an environment?

7
00:00:23,561 --> 00:00:27,081
An environment is a separate place where the application runs.

8
00:00:27,631 --> 00:00:29,317
Each one has its own servers.

9
00:00:29,867 --> 00:00:30,912
Its own data.

10
00:00:31,462 --> 00:00:32,571
Its own settings.

11
00:00:33,121 --> 00:00:35,105
And its own list of who may access it.

12
00:00:35,655 --> 00:00:39,218
Separation means a mistake in one place cannot hurt another.

13
00:00:41,268 --> 00:00:43,465
The first environment is development.

14
00:00:44,015 --> 00:00:47,685
Developers run and try the code here, often on their own machines.

15
00:00:48,235 --> 00:00:50,965
Breaking things is normal, and nobody is harmed.

16
00:00:51,515 --> 00:00:53,649
The data is fake, or a small sample.

17
00:00:54,199 --> 00:00:57,911
Development answers one question: does my change work at all?

18
00:00:59,961 --> 00:01:03,246
Next comes the test environment, often called staging.

19
00:01:03,796 --> 00:01:06,826
It is built to look as much like production as possible.

20
00:01:07,376 --> 00:01:10,064
Automated tests and Q A check the release here.

21
00:01:10,614 --> 00:01:13,686
The data is test data, never real customer data.

22
00:01:14,236 --> 00:01:17,649
Test answers a different question: will it work when it is live?

23
00:01:19,699 --> 00:01:21,022
Then there is production.

24
00:01:21,572 --> 00:01:26,308
Production is the live shop, with real customers, real orders, and real money.

25
00:01:26,858 --> 00:01:29,588
Access is limited to a few trusted people.

26
00:01:30,138 --> 00:01:33,146
Every change here is approved, recorded, and watched.

27
00:01:33,696 --> 00:01:36,086
A mistake here costs customers, and trust.

28
00:01:38,136 --> 00:01:40,781
Here is a rule that prevents many surprises.

29
00:01:41,331 --> 00:01:44,766
Build once, then move the same build through every environment.

30
00:01:45,316 --> 00:01:49,177
If you rebuild for each stage, you are no longer testing what you ship.

31
00:01:49,727 --> 00:01:54,463
What changes between environments is the settings, such as the database address.

32
00:01:55,013 --> 00:02:00,048
Secrets, like passwords, are supplied to each environment, never written into the code.

33
00:02:02,098 --> 00:02:03,229
Now, approvals.

34
00:02:03,779 --> 00:02:07,725
An approval is a recorded decision that a change may move forward.

35
00:02:08,275 --> 00:02:11,561
Code review comes first: a teammate approves the change.

36
00:02:12,111 --> 00:02:16,057
Q A signs off that it behaves as the acceptance criteria say.

37
00:02:16,607 --> 00:02:19,274
Risky changes also get a security check.

38
00:02:19,824 --> 00:02:24,197
Production needs a final release approval, from product or a release owner.

39
00:02:24,747 --> 00:02:28,715
Some gates are automatic, like tests. Others need human judgment.

40
00:02:29,265 --> 00:02:32,103
The amount of approval should match the amount of risk.

41
00:02:34,153 --> 00:02:38,420
Release risk is the chance a release causes harm, times how much harm.

42
00:02:38,970 --> 00:02:40,932
Ask four questions before you ship.

43
00:02:41,482 --> 00:02:42,805
How big is the change?

44
00:02:43,355 --> 00:02:44,934
Who is affected if it fails?

45
00:02:45,484 --> 00:02:46,828
Can we undo it quickly?

46
00:02:47,378 --> 00:02:48,807
How well was it tested?

47
00:02:49,357 --> 00:02:53,880
Fixing a typo is low risk. Changing how payments work is high risk.

48
00:02:54,430 --> 00:02:57,715
Higher risk means more checks, more care, and a better plan.

49
00:02:59,765 --> 00:03:02,389
Good teams do not just fear risk. They shrink it.

50
00:03:02,939 --> 00:03:06,651
Small changes are easier to understand, and easier to undo.

51
00:03:07,201 --> 00:03:10,572
Automated tests catch known problems before customers do.

52
00:03:11,122 --> 00:03:15,815
A gradual rollout starts with a few customers, then grows if all looks healthy.

53
00:03:16,365 --> 00:03:20,120
Choose quiet times, when the team is present and traffic is low.

54
00:03:20,670 --> 00:03:24,104
Each of these makes a failure smaller, and recovery faster.

55
00:03:26,154 --> 00:03:29,589
Even so, things go wrong. That is why rollback exists.

56
00:03:30,139 --> 00:03:33,616
A rollback returns production to the last known good version.

57
00:03:34,166 --> 00:03:34,472
Here,

58
00:03:34,472 --> 00:03:37,288
version one point four point three misbehaves,

59
00:03:37,288 --> 00:03:39,798
so we return to one point four point two.

60
00:03:40,348 --> 00:03:42,631
Rollback only works if you prepared for it.

61
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:45,165
Keep the previous version available.

62
00:03:45,715 --> 00:03:49,960
Make deployment repeatable, so going back is as easy as going forward.

63
00:03:50,510 --> 00:03:55,033
And be careful with data: some database changes cannot simply be reversed.

64
00:03:55,583 --> 00:03:57,929
Rollback is a plan you prepare, not a hope.

65
00:03:59,979 --> 00:04:02,432
Let us walk through a rollback, step by step.

66
00:04:02,982 --> 00:04:05,585
At ten o'clock, the team deploys the new version.

67
00:04:06,135 --> 00:04:09,164
Six minutes later, monitoring shows errors rising.

68
00:04:09,714 --> 00:04:12,936
The agreed rule says: if errors stay high, roll back.

69
00:04:13,486 --> 00:04:17,134
At ten fourteen, the old version is serving customers again.

70
00:04:17,684 --> 00:04:22,014
Only now does the team investigate, in a test environment, without pressure.

71
00:04:22,564 --> 00:04:27,428
Sometimes a fast fix is safer than a rollback. The team decides using the risk.

72
00:04:27,978 --> 00:04:31,946
Notice the deployment record: it says exactly what happened, and when.

73
00:04:33,996 --> 00:04:37,943
A release is not the end. Software needs care for as long as it runs.

74
00:04:38,493 --> 00:04:40,114
That care is called maintenance.

75
00:04:40,664 --> 00:04:43,992
Corrective maintenance fixes bugs that customers find.

76
00:04:44,542 --> 00:04:48,915
Adaptive maintenance keeps up with change, such as new browsers or new rules.

77
00:04:49,465 --> 00:04:52,559
Perfective maintenance improves speed, and usability.

78
00:04:53,109 --> 00:04:58,101
Preventive maintenance tidies code, and applies security patches before they are needed.

79
00:04:58,651 --> 00:05:03,301
Ignored maintenance turns into technical debt, which slows every future change.

80
00:05:03,851 --> 00:05:07,009
Planned maintenance is cheaper than emergency repair.

81
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:10,915
Watch out for four common myths.

82
00:05:11,465 --> 00:05:13,500
Myth one: if it passed in test,

83
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:14,223
it is safe.

84
00:05:14,223 --> 00:05:14,748
In fact,

85
00:05:14,748 --> 00:05:15,864
test lowers risk,

86
00:05:15,864 --> 00:05:17,374
but differences remain.

87
00:05:17,924 --> 00:05:22,575
Myth two: you can always roll back. In fact, data changes can make that hard.

88
00:05:23,125 --> 00:05:25,780
Myth three: approvals only slow us down.

89
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:26,311
In fact,

90
00:05:26,311 --> 00:05:29,098
the right ones prevent expensive mistakes.

91
00:05:29,648 --> 00:05:32,254
Myth four: maintenance is leftover work.

92
00:05:32,254 --> 00:05:32,775
In fact,

93
00:05:32,775 --> 00:05:35,707
it is where software spends most of its life.

94
00:05:36,257 --> 00:05:37,452
Plan for all four.

95
00:05:39,502 --> 00:05:40,377
Quick check.

96
00:05:40,927 --> 00:05:43,572
Name the three environments, and what each is for.

97
00:05:44,122 --> 00:05:46,319
Why build once, and move the same build?

98
00:05:46,869 --> 00:05:49,045
What four questions measure release risk?

99
00:05:49,595 --> 00:05:51,814
What must be ready before a rollback can work?

100
00:05:52,364 --> 00:05:56,524
Objective covered: MC01-O07.

101
